RNN are ideal for learning sequences.
RNNs are a special class of neural networks that operate on sequence data and allow previous outputs to be used as inputs while having hidden state.
. RNN (Vanilla Recurrent Neural Network)
🧠 Idea:
Maintains a hidden state as it processes input over time.

Each step’s output depends on current input and previous hidden state.

What is an RNN?
A Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) is a type of neural network designed to handle sequential data.

🔄 Core Idea:
RNNs remember previous inputs by using a loop in their architecture — they carry state (memory) from one step of a sequence to the next.

Think of it as a "brain with memory" that processes data step-by-step, remembering past context.

| Feature    | Feedforward NN          | RNN                        |
| ---------- | ----------------------- | -------------------------- |
| Input type | Fixed-size (e.g. image) | Sequence (e.g. text, time) |
| Memory     | No                      | Yes (remembers past info)  |
| Good for   | Images, tabular         | Text, speech, time-series  |



For each character:
You input one letter at a time (e.g. H, e, l, l, o)

The RNN:
Takes the current letter
Combines it with the hidden state (memory of the past)

Produces:
An output
A new hidden state (updated memory)
The process repeats for the entire sequence.

Input_t → [RNN cell] → Output_t
            ↑
      Hidden_{t-1}
            ↓
      Hidden_t → next step


| Use Case            | Example                      |
| ------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Text classification | Spam detection, sentiment    |
| Language modeling   | Next word prediction         |
| Speech recognition  | Voice to text                |
| Time-series         | Stock prices, weather trends |
| Music generation    | Predict next note            |



RNN Limitations
Struggles with long-term memory (past context gets diluted)

Suffers from vanishing gradients
 That’s why we often use improved versions like:

LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory)
GRU (Gated Recurrent Unit)
They include special gates to control memory flow better.


 RNN, LSTM, and GRU — three popular architectures for handling sequential data like text, speech, and time series.

 | Model    | Handles Sequences? | Remembers Long-Term? | Complexity | Speed               | Use Case Fit             |
| -------- | ------------------ | -------------------- | ---------- | ------------------- | ------------------------ |
| **RNN**  | ✅ Yes              | ❌ Poor               | 🟢 Simple  | 🟢 Fast             | Short sequences          |
| **LSTM** | ✅ Yes              | ✅ Great              | 🔴 Complex | 🔴 Slower           | Long sequences, language |
| **GRU**  | ✅ Yes              | ✅ Very Good          | 🟡 Medium  | 🟡 Faster than LSTM | Time series, NLP         |
